# LF IN THE INDEX AND LF IN THE WORKING TREE, ON EVERY PLATFORM.
#
# Git for Windows installs with core.autocrlf=true. Without this file, a
# Windows clone of this repo gets CRLF in every .sh, .json, .mjs and .ts on
# disk, and then:
#
#   - `harness verify` hashes .claude/dist/*.mjs and BUILD.json and reports
#     drift on files nobody touched;
#   - `npm run diff` compares post-state as TEXT and every case reds;
#   - `records check` recomputes seals over journal bytes that now differ;
#   - the four wall bundles are shipped as .mjs and are hashed the same way.
#
# None of that is a port defect and all of it presents as one, which makes it
# the most expensive class of bug this repo can have. `eol=lf` is stronger
# than `text=auto` alone: it pins the WORKING TREE, not only the index, so the
# bytes a Windows box reads are the bytes the seal was computed over.
#
# Verified at the commit that added this: zero tracked files carry a CR byte,
# so this normalises nothing and changes no hash today. It exists to keep that
# true.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The tree-sitter grammars behind `graph code build`. Git's binary heuristic
# would almost certainly classify these correctly on its own; "almost
# certainly" is not the standard for thirteen blobs whose ABI has to match a
# pinned parser exactly.
*.wasm binary

# Generated lockfile: LF like everything else, but never diffed by a human.
package-lock.json -diff linguist-generated
